WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.662
Why does the order matter?

2
00:00:01.662 --> 00:00:03.167
On a Category 1 instance,

3
00:00:03.167 --> 00:00:07.051
every Windows and sequel login
gets an Entra login beside it.

4
00:00:07.051 --> 00:00:09.560
The new login is proven
with a real sign-in,

5
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:14.063
and only then is the old one disabled,
observed for thirty days and dropped.

6
00:00:14.063 --> 00:00:16.350
Two mistakes here can't be taken back.

7
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:20.863
A wrong mapping locks people out, and
a dropped login can't be rolled back.

8
00:00:20.863 --> 00:00:24.942
So the plan is written as reviewable
T-SQL that executes nothing,

9
00:00:24.942 --> 00:00:28.001
and readiness is judged
from evidence, not silence.

10
00:00:28.001 --> 00:00:30.928
In this module you write a
fictional instance's plan

11
00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:33.790
and judge its parallel run
on day one and day thirty.
